36. Resource Constraints, Club of Rome and Sustainable Development
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: V | Topic: Theories of resource constraint on economic growth: Malthus to the Club of Rome, the Ricardo model and dual economy model; interrelation between population, development and environment; sustainable development; population as 'limits to growth' and as 'ultimate source'
Introduction
Can growth continue when natural resources are finite? This question has divided economists from Malthus to the modern sustainability debate.
Classical views
• Malthus: land is fixed, so diminishing returns to food production restrain population and income.
• Ricardo: as population grows, cultivation extends to inferior land, rent rises and profits fall; the economy tends toward a stationary state with zero net investment, wages at subsistence. Technical progress and trade (repeal of the Corn Laws) can delay it.
• Mill: a stationary state might be desirable, preserving environmental quality.
Dual economy model and resource limits
In the Lewis model, the traditional agricultural sector provides surplus labour and food. If agriculture stagnates, the terms of trade turn against industry, wages rise and the surplus disappears, so agriculture must grow along with industry.
Club of Rome: Limits to Growth (1972)
Meadows et al. used a system dynamics model (World3) of population, industrial output, food, resources and pollution. Business-as-usual leads to overshoot and collapse in the 21st century as non-renewable resources deplete and pollution rises. Only early policy changes, such as stabilising population and capital, lead to a sustainable equilibrium. Critics (Nordhaus, Solow, Simon): it ignores prices, substitution and technological progress; resource reserves have expanded. Supporters note later updates (Turner) match trends.
Population, development and environment
Poverty and environment interact: poor households depend on natural resources, causing deforestation and soil erosion; affluent consumption causes emissions. The Environmental Kuznets Curve suggests pollution rises then falls with income, but evidence is mixed. Climate change adds a global constraint.
Population: limits to growth versus ultimate source
• Limits to growth view (Malthus, Ehrlich): population growth strains resources and lowers living standards.
• Ultimate source view (Julian Simon, Boserup): people are the ultimate resource, generating ideas and innovation; pressure induces technological change and intensification (Boserup).
Sustainable development
The Brundtland Report (1987): development that meets present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. Concepts: weak versus strong sustainability, natural capital, Hartwick rule (invest rents from exhaustible resources in capital), green GDP, SDGs (2015).
Conclusion
Resource constraints are real, but their effect depends on technology, institutions and policy; sustainable development balances growth, equity and ecology.